RUN: %PLC -c --fpic "%S/lib_a/*.st" -o %t.a.o
RUN: %PLC -c --fpic -i "%S/lib_a/*.st" "%S/lib_b/*.st" -o %t.b.o
RUN: %PLC -c --fpic -i "%S/lib_a/*.st" -i "%S/lib_b/*.st" "%S/lib_c/*.st" -o %t.c.o
RUN: cc -shared %t.b.o %t.c.o -o %t.bc.so
RUN: nm -D --defined-only %t.bc.so | not grep -E ' __I(Shape|Base)\.'
RUN: %COMPILE -i "%S/lib_a/*.st" -i "%S/lib_b/*.st" -i "%S/lib_c/*.st" "%S/app/*.st" %t.a.o %t.b.o %t.c.o && %RUN | %CHECK %s
RUN: %COMPILE --generate-external-constructors -i "%S/lib_a/*.st" -i "%S/lib_b/*.st" -i "%S/lib_c/*.st" "%S/app/*.st" %t.a.o %t.b.o %t.c.o && %RUN | %CHECK %s

Three libraries are compiled to separate objects. lib_b includes lib_a: it
implements an interface of lib_a, extends one of its interfaces, extends its
abstract function block, function block, class and property, and has members
of its types. lib_c includes both and extends a function block of lib_b that
extends lib_a. Each object may define only the symbols of its own
declarations, so the objects of lib_b and lib_c link into one shared library
without lib_a, and the program in app/ links all three objects. The shared
library must not export the constructors of the interface parameter types of
lib_a.

CHECK: 20 30 40
CHECK: 40 ft
CHECK: 23
CHECK: 11 1 12
CHECK: 200
CHECK: child
CHECK: 92
